DECKS={
 'hw1':('commitment',[32,59,60,61,62,33,53,26,54,55,71,72,73,75,76,77,79,80,81,82,83,84]),
 'hw2':('zero-knowledge',[15,16,17,18,54,37,38,39,41,42,43,44,45,46,55,56,57,58,60,61])}
# Each exercise has its own definitions, route through the proof, and misconception check.
NOTES={
 'hw1':{
 32:('A random oracle answers each new input with an independent uniform value and remembers it for repeats. A λ-bit salt has 2<sup>λ</sup> possibilities; a λ-bit output has 2<sup>λ</sup> possible values. Hiding means the output does not help distinguish candidate messages; binding means different messages cannot share an opening.', 'Count salt guesses for hiding, then pairs of oracle queries for collisions. Compare both costs with a polynomial in λ.', 'Which cost is still exponential in λ?', ['Both 2<sup>λ</sup> and 2<sup>λ/2</sup>','Only 2<sup>λ</sup>','Neither'],0,'Halving the exponent does not turn exponential growth into polynomial growth. Collision search is faster than salt search but still exponential.'),
 60:('A PRF is an efficiently computed keyed function that looks like a random function to efficient observers. P contains efficiently decidable problems; NP contains problems with efficiently checkable witnesses. A key consistent with observed input/output pairs is such a witness.', 'Turn key consistency into a search problem, explain how P = NP makes witness search efficient, then use more observations and a fresh query to distinguish.', 'If P = NP, what becomes easy here?', ['Guessing a random-function output','Searching for a key consistent with observations','Making all keys equally likely'],1,'A candidate key can be checked efficiently. Decision-to-search self-reduction finds a consistent witness if one exists. Enough observations rule out a random function fitting any key.'),
 62:('Encryption may use randomness, so many ciphertexts can decrypt to the same plaintext. Fully homomorphic encryption evaluates a function on ciphertexts. Correctness is a statement after decryption, not a requirement that ciphertext bytes match a fresh encryption.', 'Write the required decryption equality. Then distinguish sameness of plaintext from sameness of ciphertext.', 'What must evaluation preserve?', ['The exact ciphertext bytes','The correct decrypted result','The original encryption randomness'],1,'The requirement is Dec(Eval(f,c)) = f(m). A fresh Enc(f(m)) may be a different ciphertext with the same decryption.'),
 33:('Collision resistance prevents finding two inputs with the same hash. Hiding prevents distinguishing committed messages. The random-oracle model adds ideal random-function behavior; a concrete hash does not acquire that guarantee merely by being collision resistant.', 'Separate binding from hiding. Construct a hash that leaks a message bit while retaining a collision-resistant component.', 'Does collision resistance alone establish hiding?', ['Yes, collisions and leakage are the same','No: an output may leak a bit without enabling collisions','Only for messages of one bit'],1,'A function can append the first message bit to a collision-resistant digest. Finding a collision remains hard, but the bit is visible.'),
 53:('G is cyclic with prime order q and identity 1. Raising any element to exponent 0 gives 1. A generator gives a one-to-one map from exponents in Z_q to group elements. Statistical security must withstand unlimited computation, including discrete logarithms.', 'For hiding, compare m = 0 with m = 1. For binding, allow an unbounded attacker and construct two openings with different messages.', 'For m = 0, what is H(r)^m?', ['A uniform group element','Always the identity 1','Always H(r)'],1,'Exponent zero removes all randomness. That distribution differs from the uniform group output for message 1.'),
 26:('XOR operates bit by bit: equal bits give 0, different bits give 1. It is its own inverse: a XOR b XOR b = a. A uniform λ-bit randomizer masks any fixed λ-bit message. Hiding concerns the receiver; binding concerns a sender trying to change the message.', 'First count compatible randomizers for each output. Then keep c fixed and solve r′ = c XOR m′ for a changed message.', 'For fixed c, how many messages have an opening?', ['Only the original message','Every λ-bit message','Exactly two messages'],1,'For every m′, the randomizer r′ = c XOR m′ verifies. Uniform output gives hiding; many easy openings destroy binding.'),
 54:('Pedersen computes c = g^m h^r in a group of prime order q. Here the relation h = g^x is known and x is nonzero. Arithmetic on exponents is modulo q; division by x means multiplication by its modular inverse.', 'Use c = g^(m + xr). Uniform r hides m. Keep that exponent fixed when changing m and solve for a new randomizer.', 'Knowing x destroys which property?', ['Hiding only','Binding only','Both hiding and binding'],1,'The distribution remains uniform, but the sender can compute r′ = r + (m − m′)/x mod q and open c to another message.'),
 55:('The commitment is a pair (a,b) = (g^r, g^m h^r), with h = g^x and known x. In a prime-order group, equal powers of a generator imply equal exponents modulo q. Statistical hiding must resist even an unlimited observer.', 'Compare the first components of two openings, then the second components. To test hiding, cancel the mask with b/a^x and consider discrete logs.', 'Why can two different messages not open the same pair?', ['The first component fixes r, and the second then fixes m','Randomness is secret forever','The pair has twice as many bits'],0,'Equality of g^r fixes r modulo q. Removing the same h^r from the second component fixes g^m and hence m modulo q.'),
 72:('A DDH challenge has X = g^α, Y = g^β and either Z = g^(αβ) or an independent uniform Z. The PRF is F(k,m) = H(m)^k. A simulated random oracle must give independent uniform answers at distinct inputs, and identical answers on repeated inputs.', 'Compare the proposed oracle at two distinct inputs with a true random oracle. Check the simulation before transferring any adversary advantage.', 'If H maps every distinct query to X, what gives it away?', ['Repeat queries return the same answer','Distinct queries always collide','The answers are group elements'],1,'Repeats should agree. Distinct inputs collide with probability 1/q in a true random oracle, but with probability 1 in this simulation.'),
 73:('Write X = g^α, Y = g^β and Z = g^γ. A real DDH tuple has γ = αβ modulo q; the random world samples γ independently. Multiplication of group elements adds exponents. A uniform shift of any fixed exponent remains uniform.', 'Choose r uniformly, set X′ = Xg^r and Z′ = ZY^r, then expand both exponents separately in the real and random worlds.', 'Which transformed Z preserves the real relation?', ['Zg^r','ZY^r','ZX'],1,'The new exponent of X is α + r. The real Z exponent must therefore be β(α + r), obtained by multiplying Z by Y^r.'),
 75:('Use the re-randomization X′ = Xg^r, Z′ = ZY^r from part (b). For every distinct message m_i, store one independent r_i and H(m_i) = Xg^r_i. The adversary is allowed exactly one PRF query in this part.', 'Keep an oracle table. Return ZY^r_j for the chosen PRF input. Show the real world uses key β and the random world supplies one independent uniform output.', 'What should happen when H receives a repeated input?', ['Sample a fresh answer','Return the stored answer','Abort the proof'],1,'A random oracle is a function: repeated inputs must agree. Fresh randomness is used once per distinct input and then cached.'),
 77:('Part (c) supplies only one PRF output. For many outputs, each answer must be jointly consistent with the real keyed function or jointly independent in the random world. Use H(m_i) = X^u_i g^v_i and f(m_i) = Z^u_i Y^v_i with fresh independent u_i,v_i per distinct message.', 'Expand the exponent pair as a 2-by-2 linear map. Its determinant αβ − γ decides whether two independent random inputs give a uniform output pair.', 'Why are two independent scalars useful?', ['They make each single answer longer','They can remove correlations between oracle and function outputs','They avoid storing repeated inputs'],1,'An invertible map on Z_q² sends a uniform pair to a uniform pair. If the determinant vanishes, independence fails; that bad event has probability 1/q in the random DDH world.'),
 81:('A vector commitment binds indexed messages m_1,…,m_n to a public value. An opening for position i must verify m_i at that position; hiding is not required in this exercise. The given construction treats each vector coordinate independently.', 'Specify exactly what is published and revealed. Then explain why replacing one coordinate would violate binding at that coordinate.', 'What must a position opening bind?', ['A value somewhere in the vector','The value at the claimed index','Only the vector length'],1,'Both the index and its value matter. A valid opening for a different position does not verify the requested coordinate.'),
 82:('A collision-resistant hash makes it hard to find different complete inputs with the same digest. To bind a vector with one digest, hash an unambiguous encoding of all indexed entries. A verifier must recompute that digest from enough opening information.', 'Commit to the whole vector, describe the full opening needed for recomputation, and distinguish short commitment size from opening size.', 'Does one short digest guarantee short openings?', ['Yes','No: verification may require the whole vector','Only if n is a power of two'],1,'Hashing the entire vector compresses the commitment, but a straightforward verifier still needs all entries to recompute the hash.'),
 84:('A binary Merkle tree hashes leaves into ordered parent pairs, ending at one root. To authenticate index i, give its value and one sibling hash per level. The index determines whether each sibling goes on the left or the right.', 'Hash the claimed leaf, combine siblings in index order until reaching the root, then locate the first differing pair if a false opening is accepted.', 'Why is a Merkle opening logarithmic?', ['It reveals one sibling per tree level','It reveals every leaf','The root contains all leaves in plain text'],0,'A balanced tree on n leaves has log₂ n levels. One sibling per level reconstructs the path to the root without revealing every other leaf.')},
 'hw2':{
 16:('P means efficiently decidable; NP means a short witness can be checked efficiently. If P = NP, a verifier can decide every NP statement directly. An empty transcript contains no prover communication to simulate.', 'Use the hypothetical efficient decision algorithm. Check completeness and soundness from its correct decision, then simulate the empty transcript.', 'If P = NP, why is an empty proof enough?', ['The verifier can decide membership directly','Every statement becomes true','The prover reveals its witness'],0,'P = NP would supply an efficient decision algorithm for every NP language. The verifier uses it, and there is no transcript to leak a witness.'),
 17:('Perfect completeness promises acceptance for a true statement when both parties follow the protocol. A malicious verifier can choose an arbitrary strategy, including always rejecting. Completeness is different from zero knowledge, which concerns the information in the verifier view.', 'Identify the honest-party condition in completeness. Then give a verifier strategy that violates the proposed universal claim.', 'Can a malicious verifier always reject a true statement?', ['Yes; completeness assumes an honest verifier','No; perfect completeness forces acceptance','Only with probability 1/3'],0,'The verifier controls its own final output. Always rejecting does not contradict a guarantee about an honest verifier.'),
 18:('Zero knowledge protects the witness of an honest prover following the protocol. A malicious prover is free to send extra information. Soundness restricts false claims being accepted, not voluntary disclosure of a prover secret.', 'Check whose honesty the privacy definition assumes. Exhibit a prover that sends the entire witness to the honest verifier.', 'Can a malicious prover send its witness in the clear?', ['No; zero knowledge blocks the message','Yes; the honest-prover privacy guarantee does not forbid deliberate leakage','Only if soundness fails'],1,'The prover can voluntarily reveal the witness. Zero knowledge describes the prescribed honest prover behavior; it does not force every malicious prover to keep secrets.'),
 54:('P is the class of languages with efficient membership decision algorithms. Non-interactive means no challenge–response exchange. A zero-knowledge simulator reproduces the communication without needing a witness.', 'Let the verifier decide membership directly. Check completeness, soundness and simulation for the empty transcript.', 'How many prover messages are necessary for a language in P?', ['At least three','None: the verifier can decide membership','One secret witness'],1,'An empty transcript is non-interactive and trivially simulatable. The decision algorithm supplies completeness and soundness.'),
 39:('The graph protocol has a one-bit challenge and at most 1/2 cheating success per round. Sequential repetition completes one interaction before starting the next. Zero knowledge requires a simulator for the entire verifier view, including the state carried from one round to the next.', 'Multiply conditional success bounds for soundness. For privacy, simulate each round against the verifier state produced by the preceding simulated rounds.', 'With four fresh rounds, the cheating bound is…', ['1/2','1/16','1/4'],1,'Acceptance requires all four successes. Under the per-round conditional bound, (1/2)^4 = 1/16.'),
 41:('Parallel repetition sends all first messages, then the entire challenge vector, then all responses. A malicious verifier can choose that vector jointly from the first messages. The graph-protocol simulator can prepare to answer one anticipated challenge per copy.', 'Ask how many challenge bits must be guessed simultaneously. Compare the expected retry cost with polynomial time.', 'Guessing a k-bit challenge vector may require how many trials on average?', ['2k','2^k','k²'],1,'A full correct guess has probability 2^−k. Its reciprocal, 2^k, is exponential. This shows why that simulation argument fails; it does not prove that every parallel protocol leaks.'),
 42:('A Sigma transcript consists of a first message, challenge and response. Honest-verifier zero knowledge gives a simulator with the honest execution’s transcript distribution. Here honest challenges in separate copies are sampled independently.', 'Generate independent simulated transcripts, then group their first messages, challenges and responses into three parallel batches.', 'Why can we combine single-copy simulators here?', ['Honest independent challenges give the required joint distribution','All malicious verifiers behave honestly','Soundness implies privacy'],0,'Independent simulation matches the product distribution of independent honest executions. The reasoning does not cover arbitrary correlated malicious challenges.'),
 44:('Schnorr uses a prime-order group with generator g and public h = g^x. The secret is x. The prover samples r, sends u = g^r, receives challenge c, and sends z = r + cx modulo q. Verification checks g^z = u h^c.', 'Substitute z into g^z. Split the exponent sum, then replace g^r by u and g^x by h.', 'What establishes perfect completeness?', ['The verification equality holds for every honest r and c','The verifier guesses the secret','The challenge is always zero'],0,'The equality follows algebraically: g^(r+cx) = g^r (g^x)^c = u h^c, for every honest choice.'),
 45:('The Schnorr verifier sees (u,c,z) satisfying g^z = u h^c. The simulator knows public g,h,q but not secret x. In an honest run, c is uniform and independent; for fixed c, uniform r makes z = r + cx uniform too.', 'Sample c and z first, solve u = g^z h^(−c), then compare the joint distribution to an honest transcript.', 'What does the simulator compute last?', ['The secret x','The first message u','The group order q'],1,'It generates the transcript in reverse order. The public verification equation uniquely determines u from c and z, so no secret is needed.'),
 46:('Rewinding restores a prover to the saved state after the first message u. Two accepted Schnorr transcripts with that same u obey g^z = u h^c and g^z′ = u h^c′. Exponent differences and inverses are taken modulo prime q.', 'Divide the accepting equations to cancel u. If c differs from c′, invert c − c′ to recover x. Count the probability that two uniform challenges coincide.', 'What breaks extraction from two transcripts?', ['Different responses','Equal challenges','The same first message'],1,'Equal challenges make c − c′ zero, which has no inverse. The same first message is required to cancel the reused randomness.'),
 57:('A Boolean wire holds a bit. XOR outputs 1 for unequal inputs; AND outputs 1 only for two 1s. A satisfiable circuit has some input assignment producing output 1. Publish one binding, hiding commitment per wire and reuse it wherever that wire appears.', 'Evaluate a satisfying assignment, commit to every wire, batch the per-gate Sigma messages, then verify all gates and open the final output as 1.', 'How do adjacent gate proofs agree on a shared wire?', ['Reuse one commitment for that wire','Use unrelated commitments','Check only the final output'],0,'Binding fixes the bit in the shared commitment. Independent commitments could let adjacent gates claim incompatible values.'),
 58:('Perfect binding fixes at most one bit per wire commitment. If all local gate relations are true, those bits describe a valid circuit evaluation. An unsatisfiable circuit cannot have a valid evaluation with final output 1.', 'Assume acceptance of a false circuit, locate a false gate relation, and use a union bound over the polynomial number N of gates.', 'If acceptance probability is ε, some gate attack has probability at least…', ['ε/N','ε^N','N/ε'],0,'If N nonnegative probabilities sum to at least ε, at least one is ≥ ε/N. Dividing a non-negligible probability by polynomial N keeps it non-negligible.'),
 61:('The simulator must imitate wire commitments, gate transcripts and the final output opening without a satisfying assignment. Hiding can conceal changes in wire bits. Ordinary honest-verifier zero knowledge guarantees simulation on true gate statements; it does not automatically cover false ones.', 'State the proposed fake assignment and the hybrids. Then identify exactly which simulator guarantee is needed when a fake gate relation is false.', 'If all wire bits are 1, is an XOR gate statement true?', ['Yes, 1 XOR 1 = 1','No, 1 XOR 1 = 0','It depends on the commitment randomness'],1,'The proposed fake assignment can make an XOR statement false. A rigorous proof needs a simulator or hybrid argument that supports those generated statements; ordinary true-statement HVZK alone is insufficient.')}
}
def checkpoint(data):
 knowledge,route,question,answers,correct,feedback=data
 return '<aside class="learning homework-check" data-correct="'+str(correct)+'"><div class="lab-eyebrow">Check your reasoning</div><h3>'+question+'</h3><div class="lab-actions">'+''.join('<button type="button" data-answer="'+str(i)+'">'+a+'</button>' for i,a in enumerate(answers))+'</div><output class="lab-result" aria-live="polite">Choose an answer, then use the explanation to check the step-by-step solution.</output><details class="checkpoint-explanation"><summary>Explain the answer</summary><p>'+feedback+'</p></details></aside>'
for deck,(source,order) in DECKS.items():
 raw=(ROOT/'docs'/source/'index.html').read_text();matches=list(re.finditer(r'<section\b.*?</section>',raw,re.S));by_id={int(match[1]):m[0] for m in matches if (match:=re.search(r'id="lesson-(\d+)"',m[0]))}
 prefix=raw[:matches[0].start()];suffix=raw[matches[-1].end():]
 prefix=prefix.replace(f'data-chapter="{source}"',f'data-chapter="{deck}"').replace('<title>PETS — '+('Commitment' if source=='commitment' else 'Zero Knowledge')+'</title>',f'<title>PETS — {deck.upper()}</title>')
 prefix=re.sub(r'<meta content="[^"]*" name="description"/>',f'<meta content="PETS {deck.upper()}: self-contained exercises, prerequisite notes, worked solutions and interactive learning." name="description"/>',prefix)
 cards=[]
 for n,old in enumerate(order,1):
  c=by_id[old];c=c.replace(f'id="lesson-{old}"',f'id="lesson-{n}" data-source-card="{old}" data-source-chapter="{source}"')
  prev=f'<a aria-label="Previous card" class="navbtn" href="#lesson-{n-1}"><svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="m14.5 6.5-5.5 5.5 5.5 5.5"/></svg></a>' if n>1 else '<span class="navbtn" aria-disabled="true" aria-label="Previous card">‹</span>'
  nxt=f'<a aria-label="Next card" class="navbtn" href="#lesson-{n+1}"><svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="m9.5 6.5 5.5 5.5-5.5 5.5"/></svg></a>' if n<len(order) else ''
  c=re.sub(r'<nav aria-label="Card navigation".*?</nav>',f'<nav aria-label="Card navigation" class="nav">{prev}{nxt}</nav>',c,flags=re.S)
  if old in NOTES[deck]:
   c=c.replace('</article>',checkpoint(NOTES[deck][old])+'</article>')
  cards.append(c)
 suffix=re.sub(r'1 / \d+',f'1 / {len(order)}',suffix);suffix=re.sub(r'Card 1 of \d+',f'Card 1 of {len(order)}',suffix);suffix=re.sub(r'max="\d+"',f'max="{len(order)}"',suffix)
 suffix=suffix.replace('</body>','<script src="../homework.js" defer></script></body>')
 path=ROOT/'docs'/deck;path.mkdir(exist_ok=True);(path/'index.html').write_text(prefix+''.join(cards)+suffix)
 print(deck,len(cards),'cards',len(NOTES[deck]),'self-contained exercises')
